1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,845
Where do you start designing a feature: the database, the API, or the code?

2
00:00:04,845 --> 00:00:11,345
We start with the domain: the things that exist, the states they move through, and the rules that never break.

3
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:18,023
First comes a one-page sketch of the whole system: the main entities, and which module owns each one.

4
00:00:18,023 --> 00:00:24,357
Links between modules are only IDs, never foreign keys, so a module can move out on its own later.

5
00:00:24,357 --> 00:00:31,952
Second comes the contract: an OpenAPI spec in the platform folder, which promises clients exactly what they can ask for.

6
00:00:31,952 --> 00:00:40,011
Gradle turns it into a partner API jar, and our controller must implement it, so the code can never drift from the spec.

7
00:00:40,011 --> 00:00:44,904
Third comes the database: a Liquibase migration in the partner's own schema.

8
00:00:44,904 --> 00:00:52,011
The partner module has its own data source, and connects as a database user that sees nothing but its own schema.

9
00:00:52,011 --> 00:01:00,511
Then the code: the controller calls the service, the service calls the repository, and a mapper turns the entity into the contract's model.

10
00:01:00,511 --> 00:01:06,571
A missing partner is not a crash: the error starter returns a problem detail with the code partner not found.

11
00:01:06,571 --> 00:01:14,535
A unit test guards the rule, and an integration test on a real Postgres proves the partner user cannot touch the payment schema.

12
00:01:14,535 --> 00:01:19,761
Swagger UI shows the exact spec we wrote, not documentation guessed from the code.

13
00:01:19,761 --> 00:01:28,142
A k6 load test reads partners at about two thousand four hundred requests per second, with a p95 of three milliseconds.

14
00:01:28,142 --> 00:01:34,547
Domain, contract, database, code, and test: every episode follows this order.

15
00:01:34,547 --> 00:01:39,369
All of this code is in the ecom repository at the tag ep zero three.

16
00:01:39,369 --> 00:01:44,416
Next, the first real feature: a merchant signs up and proves they own their email.

